# vendor_init property writes.
#
# /vendor/build.prop -- which the build appends device/xsh/k50sv1_64_bsp/vendor.prop
# to (build/make/core/Makefile:489) -- and every setprop in a /vendor rc file are
# evaluated in the vendor_init context, not init:
#   system/core/init/subcontext.cpp:56-58   {"/vendor", kVendorContext="u:r:vendor_init:s0"}
#   system/core/init/property_service.cpp:656-663, 726
#       LoadProperties() picks that context by path prefix and then calls
#       CheckPermissions(), which is a real selinux_check_access() on
#       <type>:property_service:set.
#
# INERT ON THIS PRODUCT, AND KEPT ON PURPOSE. This block is the canonical
# explanation for all ten set_prop(vendor_init, ...) rules in the tree; the other
# nine sites point back to it rather than repeat it.
#
# What this comment used to say was that system/sepolicy/public/vendor_init.te:
# 220-246 grants vendor_init "a fixed list of PLATFORM property types only", so a
# device-defined type needs its own rule or the assignment is dropped with
#   init: Do not have permissions to set <key> ... in property file /vendor/build.prop
# That is FALSE for this product, and it matters because it was used to justify
# ten rules and to explain a boot failure that never happened. Lines 220-246 are
# only the tail of that file. Ahead of them, at :193-215, sits
#
#     not_compatible_property(`
#       set_prop(vendor_init, { property_type -<18 platform exceptions> })
#     ')
#
# and not_compatible_property (system/sepolicy/public/te_macros:498) is
#     define(`not_compatible_property',
#            `ifelse(target_compatible_property, `true', , $1)')
# i.e. it emits its argument whenever target_compatible_property is NOT `true'.
# PRODUCT_COMPATIBLE_PROPERTY is false here -- build/make/core/config.mk:645-651
# leaves it false for PRODUCT_SHIPPING_API_LEVEL <= 27 and this product is at 26
# (see BoardConfig.mk, which relies on the same fact for its `on property:`
# triggers). So the block EXPANDS, and vendor_init already holds
# property_service:set on every property_type except those 18 -- all of which are
# platform types (device_config_*, restorecon_prop, netd_stable_secret_prop,
# firstboot_prop, pm_prop, *_boot_reason_prop, apexd_prop, gsid_prop,
# nnapi_ext_deny_product_prop). Not one MTK type is among them.
#
# Verified against this product's own build output rather than by reading:
# out/target/product/k50sv1_64_bsp/obj/ETC/sepolicy_neverallows_intermediates/
# policy.conf carries the expanded `allow vendor_init { property_type -... }:
# property_service set;` at the #line 193 marker, and with every one of the ten
# rules deleted checkpolicy still reports the permission as granted.
#
# They are kept because they stop being inert the moment PRODUCT_SHIPPING_API_LEVEL
# is raised above 27: that flips PRODUCT_COMPATIBLE_PROPERTY to true, :193-215
# stops expanding, vendor_init drops to the 27-type list at :220-246, and every
# key below would start failing with exactly the "Do not have permissions to set"
# line the old comment described. Deleting them would turn raising the shipping
# API level from a no-op into a silent radio/GNSS/power/WMT regression that first
# appears as a missing property. Stock ships the same shape: its vendor_init_29_0
# carries property_service set on ~90 MTK property types.
#
# The rule for a NEW device property type is therefore unchanged -- add a
# matching set_prop(vendor_init, ...) in that type's directory -- only the reason
# is different: it is insurance, not a fix.

# vendor.prop: ro.vendor.mtk_gps_support, ro.vendor.mtk_agps_app,
#              ro.vendor.mtk_log_hide_gps
set_prop(vendor_init, mtk_gnss_ro_prop)
